from lib import *
D="d5"

L("5-01",D,"d5c1","Security Concepts and Security Program Elements",["5.1","5.2"],30,
"Security is about protecting confidentiality, integrity, and availability (the CIA triad). You need to understand what threatens a network, how weaknesses get exploited, and which policies and training reduce risk.",
[(B,"Core vocabulary","A vulnerability is a weakness. A threat is a potential danger that could exploit it. An exploit is the actual method or code used to take advantage of a vulnerability. Mitigation is the action that reduces the risk."),
 (I,"Common attacks","DoS/DDoS (overwhelm resources), spoofing (fake IP/MAC/ARP/DHCP identity), reflection and amplification (spoof the victim's address so responses flood it), man-in-the-middle (intercept traffic, e.g., ARP poisoning), reconnaissance (scanning to gather information), malware (virus needs a host file and user action, worm self-propagates, trojan disguises itself, ransomware encrypts data for ransom), social engineering (phishing, spear phishing, whaling, vishing by voice, smishing by SMS, pretexting, tailgating), and password attacks (dictionary, brute force)."),
 (X,"Security program elements","User awareness (making everyone aware of threats and policies, e.g., phishing simulations), user training (formal instruction for specific roles), and physical access control (badges, locks, cameras, mantraps protecting network rooms). Technical controls are useless if anyone can walk into the wiring closet and plug in a console cable.")],
"Most breaches begin with people or simple misconfigurations, not sophisticated exploits. CCNA-level engineers are the first line of defense.",
[("Phishing","An email impersonating IT asks users to 'verify' passwords on a fake portal. Awareness training and MFA reduce the impact."),
 ("Amplification","Attackers send small spoofed DNS queries; large responses flood the victim.")],
["Using 'threat' and 'vulnerability' interchangeably.",
 "Thinking a worm needs user action to spread.",
 "Neglecting physical security."],
[("CIA triad","Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability."),
 ("Exploit","Mechanism that takes advantage of a vulnerability."),
 ("MITM","Man-in-the-middle: attacker intercepts and possibly alters traffic."),
 ("Spear phishing / Whaling","Targeted phishing / phishing aimed at executives.")],
["Vulnerability = weakness; threat = potential danger; exploit = the attack method; mitigation = the countermeasure.",
 "Program elements: user awareness, user training, physical access control."],
["Expect matching questions: attack name ↔ description."],
"Security protects the CIA triad. Know vulnerability/threat/exploit/mitigation, common attack types, and the human and physical elements of a security program.",
[("A weakness in software that could be attacked is a(n):",["Threat","Exploit","Vulnerability","Mitigation"],2,"A vulnerability is a weakness."),
 ("Which malware spreads by itself without user action?",["Virus","Worm","Trojan","Phishing"],1,"Worms self-replicate across networks."),
 ("Phishing by phone call is called:",["Smishing","Vishing","Whaling","Pharming"],1,"Voice phishing = vishing.")])

L("5-02",D,"d5c1","Device Access Control, Passwords, and AAA",["5.3","5.4","5.8"],35,
"Every router and switch must be protected so only authorized administrators can log in, and their actions should be controlled and recorded. Strong password policies, multifactor authentication, and AAA with central servers make that possible.",
[(B,"Local passwords","Console: line console 0 / password X / login. VTY: line vty 0 4 / password X / login (or 'login local' with usernames). Privileged EXEC: 'enable secret X' (hashed; preferred over 'enable password', and if both exist, enable secret is used). 'username admin secret X' creates a local user with a hashed password. 'service password-encryption' applies weak type 7 encryption to clear-text passwords in the config; it only prevents shoulder-surfing."),
 (I,"Password policies and alternatives","Policies cover length, complexity, expiration, reuse, and lockout ('login block-for 120 attempts 3 within 60'). Alternatives and additions: multifactor authentication (MFA) combining something you know, have, and are; digital certificates (PKI) proving identity cryptographically; biometrics (fingerprint, face). Also set 'exec-timeout' to log out idle sessions."),
 (X,"AAA, RADIUS, and TACACS+","Authentication (who are you), Authorization (what may you do), Accounting (what did you do). Enable with 'aaa new-model'. RADIUS: open standard, UDP 1812/1813 (legacy 1645/1646), encrypts only the password, combines authentication and authorization; common for network access (802.1X, VPN users). TACACS+: Cisco-developed, TCP 49, encrypts the entire payload, separates all three A's, supports per-command authorization; preferred for device administration. Cisco ISE can act as both. Always keep a local fallback: 'aaa authentication login default group tacacs+ local'.")],
"Compromised device credentials give an attacker the whole network. AAA provides accountability and centralized control over who changes what.",
[("Secure login","enable secret, local users with secret, login local on VTY, SSH only, exec-timeout 10, login block-for to slow brute force."),
 ("Central admin","All switches authenticate administrators via TACACS+ on ISE; helpdesk staff are authorized only for show commands; every command is logged by accounting.")],
["Using 'enable password' instead of 'enable secret'.",
 "Believing 'service password-encryption' is strong encryption.",
 "Configuring AAA without a local fallback and locking yourself out.",
 "Mixing up RADIUS (UDP) and TACACS+ (TCP 49)."],
[("AAA","Authentication, Authorization, Accounting."),
 ("RADIUS","Standard AAA protocol, UDP 1812/1813, encrypts password only."),
 ("TACACS+","Cisco AAA protocol, TCP 49, full-payload encryption."),
 ("MFA","Multifactor authentication using two or more factor types.")],
["enable secret beats enable password; type 7 is reversible.",
 "RADIUS: UDP 1812/1813, password-only encryption, combines authN/authZ.",
 "TACACS+: TCP 49, full encryption, separates AAA, per-command authorization.",
 "MFA factors: know, have, are."],
["Comparison tables of RADIUS vs TACACS+ are very common."],
"Protect device access with hashed secrets, local users, and timeouts; strengthen authentication with password policy, MFA, certificates, or biometrics; and centralize control with AAA using RADIUS or TACACS+.",
[("Which protocol encrypts the entire packet body?",["RADIUS","TACACS+","SNMPv2c","Telnet"],1,"TACACS+ encrypts the full payload."),
 ("Which command stores the privileged EXEC password as a hash?",["enable password","enable secret","service password-encryption","password 7"],1,"enable secret."),
 ("Which TCP port does TACACS+ use?",["49","1812","1645","22"],0,"TACACS+ = TCP 49.")])

L("5-03",D,"d5c2","Access Control Lists (ACLs)",["5.6"],45,
"An ACL is an ordered list of permit and deny rules. Routers use ACLs to filter traffic on interfaces and to identify traffic for features like NAT. Every packet is checked against the list top to bottom; the first match decides.",
[(B,"How ACLs evaluate","Rules are checked in order. The first matching rule's action is applied and no more rules are checked. If nothing matches, the invisible implicit 'deny any' at the end drops the packet. An ACL with only deny statements therefore blocks everything."),
 (I,"Standard vs extended, and wildcard masks","Standard ACLs (1-99, 1300-1999) match only the source IP. Extended ACLs (100-199, 2000-2699) match protocol, source, destination, and ports. Named ACLs work the same way with names. Wildcard masks use 0 = must match, 1 = ignore: 0.0.0.255 matches a /24; 'host 10.1.1.1' = 10.1.1.1 0.0.0.0; 'any' = 0.0.0.0 255.255.255.255. Wildcard for a subnet = 255.255.255.255 - subnet mask (e.g., /26 → 0.0.0.63)."),
 (X,"Configuration, placement and verification","Numbered standard: access-list 10 permit 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255. Named extended: ip access-list extended WEB / 10 permit tcp 10.1.1.0 0.0.0.255 host 10.2.2.10 eq 443 / 20 deny ip any host 10.2.2.10 / 30 permit ip any any. Apply: interface Gi0/1 / ip access-group WEB in. Rules: one ACL per interface, per direction, per protocol (IPv4/IPv6). Place extended ACLs close to the source (drop unwanted traffic early); place standard ACLs close to the destination (they cannot distinguish destinations). Named ACLs allow inserting/deleting individual sequence numbers. Restrict VTY access with 'access-class 5 in' under line vty. Verify: 'show access-lists' (with match counters), 'show ip interface Gi0/1'.")],
"ACLs are the most common packet-filtering and traffic-identification tool on Cisco devices and appear in many exam scenarios.",
[("Block one host from a server","ip access-list extended BLOCK / deny ip host 10.1.1.50 host 10.2.2.10 / permit ip any any — applied inbound on the interface closest to 10.1.1.50."),
 ("Wildcard calculation","Permit 172.16.32.0/20: wildcard = 0.0.15.255 → access-list 20 permit 172.16.32.0 0.0.15.255.")],
["Forgetting the implicit deny (ending an ACL with only denies).",
 "Ordering a broad permit above a specific deny, so the deny never matches.",
 "Applying in the wrong direction (in vs out are from the router interface's perspective).",
 "Using a subnet mask instead of a wildcard mask."],
[("Implicit deny","Hidden final rule denying all unmatched traffic."),
 ("Wildcard mask","Inverse mask: 0 bits must match, 1 bits are ignored."),
 ("Inbound / Outbound","Filtering as traffic enters / leaves the interface."),
 ("Sequence number","Line number in an ACL used for editing.")],
["Standard 1-99, 1300-1999 (source only) → near destination.",
 "Extended 100-199, 2000-2699 (src, dst, protocol, ports) → near source.",
 "Top-down, first match, implicit deny any.",
 "One ACL per interface, per direction, per protocol."],
["Typical questions: which ACL line matches, what traffic is blocked, or which wildcard matches a range.",
 "access-class applies to VTY lines; access-group applies to interfaces."],
"ACLs filter traffic using ordered permit/deny statements with an implicit deny. Use wildcard masks, place standard ACLs near the destination and extended near the source, and verify with show access-lists.",
[("What wildcard mask matches 10.10.8.0/22?",["0.0.3.255","0.0.4.255","255.255.252.0","0.0.7.255"],0,"/22 mask 255.255.252.0 → wildcard 0.0.3.255."),
 ("Where should a standard ACL usually be placed?",["Close to the source","Close to the destination","On every interface","Only on VTY lines"],1,"Standard ACLs match only source, so place them near the destination."),
 ("An ACL contains only 'deny host 10.1.1.1'. What happens to traffic from 10.1.1.2?",["Permitted","Denied by implicit deny","Logged only","Forwarded to CPU"],1,"No permit exists, so the implicit deny drops it.")],
diagrams=["acl_flow"])

L("5-04",D,"d5c2","Layer 2 Security: Port Security, DHCP Snooping, and DAI",["5.7"],40,
"Attacks inside the LAN often target Layer 2: plugging in unauthorized devices, running rogue DHCP servers, or poisoning ARP caches. Port security, DHCP snooping, and Dynamic ARP Inspection stop these attacks at the access switch.",
[(B,"Port security","Limits which and how many MAC addresses can use a port. Enable on an access (or static trunk) port: switchport mode access / switchport port-security / switchport port-security maximum 2 / switchport port-security mac-address sticky. Default maximum is 1 MAC and default violation mode is shutdown."),
 (I,"Violation modes and recovery","Shutdown: port goes err-disabled, increments violation counter, sends log/SNMP (default). Restrict: drops offending frames, logs, increments counter. Protect: drops offending frames silently (no log, no counter). Recover from err-disabled with 'shutdown' then 'no shutdown' or automatically with 'errdisable recovery cause psecure-violation'. Verify: 'show port-security interface Gi0/5', 'show port-security address'. Sticky addresses are added to the running config."),
 (X,"DHCP snooping and DAI","DHCP snooping ('ip dhcp snooping', 'ip dhcp snooping vlan 10') classifies ports as trusted (uplinks toward legitimate DHCP servers: 'ip dhcp snooping trust') or untrusted (default). Untrusted ports drop DHCP server messages (OFFER, ACK, NAK), blocking rogue servers, and the switch builds a binding table (MAC, IP, VLAN, port, lease). Optional rate limiting: 'ip dhcp snooping limit rate 10'. Note: when snooping inserts Option 82, some IOS DHCP servers reject it; 'no ip dhcp snooping information option' is a common fix. Dynamic ARP Inspection ('ip arp inspection vlan 10') checks ARP messages on untrusted ports against the DHCP snooping binding table (or ARP ACLs for static hosts) and drops mismatches, preventing ARP spoofing/poisoning. Trust uplinks with 'ip arp inspection trust'. Untrusted ports are rate limited to 15 ARP packets per second by default. Optional 'ip arp inspection validate src-mac dst-mac ip'.")],
"These features stop common, easy LAN attacks such as rogue DHCP servers (traffic redirection) and ARP poisoning (man-in-the-middle).",
[("Conference room port","maximum 1, sticky, violation restrict: a visitor's laptop is blocked and logged without shutting the port."),
 ("Rogue DHCP","A user plugs in a home router. DHCP snooping drops its OFFERs on the untrusted port; clients still get addresses from the real server via the trusted uplink.")],
["Enabling DAI without DHCP snooping (DAI needs the binding table or ARP ACLs).",
 "Forgetting to trust the uplink toward the DHCP server, so all clients fail.",
 "Enabling port security on a dynamic (DTP) port — IOS rejects it.",
 "Mixing up protect (silent) and restrict (logs)."],
[("Err-disabled","Port state disabled by the switch due to an error/violation."),
 ("Sticky MAC","Dynamically learned MAC saved into the running configuration."),
 ("Binding table","DHCP snooping record of MAC, IP, VLAN, interface, lease."),
 ("ARP poisoning","Sending false ARP replies to redirect traffic.")],
["Port security defaults: max 1, violation shutdown.",
 "Modes: protect (drop silently), restrict (drop + log + counter), shutdown (err-disable).",
 "DHCP snooping: untrusted ports drop server messages; trust uplinks.",
 "DAI uses the DHCP snooping binding table; default 15 pps limit on untrusted ports."],
["Expect a 'show port-security interface' output and questions about the violation mode or counter."],
"Port security restricts MAC addresses per port, DHCP snooping blocks rogue DHCP servers and builds a binding table, and DAI uses that table to stop ARP spoofing.",
[("Which violation mode drops frames and logs but keeps the port up?",["protect","restrict","shutdown","sticky"],1,"restrict drops, logs, and increments the counter."),
 ("DHCP snooping drops which messages on untrusted ports?",["DISCOVER","REQUEST","OFFER and ACK","All ARP"],2,"Server-originated messages such as OFFER/ACK/NAK are dropped."),
 ("What does DAI validate ARP packets against?",["MAC address table","DHCP snooping binding table","Routing table","CDP table"],1,"DAI uses the binding table (or ARP ACLs).")],
diagrams=["l2_security"])

L("5-05",D,"d5c3","VPN Fundamentals",["5.5"],30,
"A VPN creates a private, encrypted tunnel across a public network such as the internet. Site-to-site VPNs connect whole offices; remote-access VPNs connect individual users to the corporate network.",
[(B,"Why VPNs","The internet is cheap and everywhere but untrusted. A VPN protects data so that branch offices and home workers can use the internet as if it were a private link."),
 (I,"IPsec services","IPsec provides confidentiality (encryption, e.g., AES), integrity (hashing, e.g., SHA), authentication (pre-shared keys or certificates), and anti-replay. ESP (IP protocol 50) provides encryption plus integrity; AH (IP protocol 51) provides integrity/authentication only and is rarely used. IKE negotiates keys on UDP 500; NAT traversal uses UDP 4500. Tunnel mode encrypts the entire original packet and adds a new IP header (typical for site-to-site)."),
 (X,"Site-to-site vs remote access","Site-to-site: VPN gateways (routers/firewalls) at each site build a permanent tunnel; hosts are unaware of it. GRE over IPsec adds support for multicast/routing protocols. Remote access: a client (e.g., Cisco Secure Client, formerly AnyConnect) on the user's device builds a tunnel on demand using TLS/SSL or IPsec; clientless SSL VPN uses only a web browser for limited access. v1.1 objective 5.5 asks you to describe IPsec remote access and site-to-site VPNs; configuration is not required.")],
"Hybrid work and branch connectivity depend on VPNs. Understanding them helps you troubleshoot tunnels and choose the right design.",
[("Branch to HQ","Two firewalls build an IPsec site-to-site tunnel over the internet, replacing an expensive leased line."),
 ("Remote worker","An employee launches the VPN client, authenticates with MFA, and receives an internal IP address.")],
["Thinking hosts must run VPN software for site-to-site VPNs.",
 "Believing GRE alone encrypts traffic (it does not).",
 "Confusing ESP (encrypts) with AH (no encryption)."],
[("IPsec","Framework for securing IP traffic."),
 ("ESP / AH","Encapsulating Security Payload (encrypts) / Authentication Header (no encryption)."),
 ("IKE","Internet Key Exchange; negotiates security associations."),
 ("Remote-access VPN","Per-user tunnel from a client to the corporate network.")],
["IPsec: confidentiality, integrity, authentication, anti-replay.",
 "ESP = protocol 50; AH = protocol 51; IKE = UDP 500; NAT-T = UDP 4500.",
 "Site-to-site = gateway-to-gateway, always on; remote access = client-based, on demand."],
["Expect comparison questions between site-to-site and remote-access VPNs."],
"VPNs secure traffic across untrusted networks. IPsec provides confidentiality, integrity, authentication, and anti-replay for site-to-site tunnels; remote-access VPNs use client software with TLS or IPsec.",
[("Which IPsec protocol provides encryption?",["AH","ESP","GRE","IKE"],1,"ESP encrypts; AH does not."),
 ("In a site-to-site VPN, which devices build the tunnel?",["End-user PCs","VPN gateways such as routers or firewalls","Switches","DNS servers"],1,"Gateways build site-to-site tunnels."),
 ("Which port does IKE use?",["UDP 500","TCP 443","UDP 4500 only","TCP 22"],0,"IKE uses UDP 500 (4500 with NAT-T).")],
diagrams=["vpn"])

L("5-06",D,"d5c3","Wireless Security Protocols",["5.9","5.10"],25,
"Wireless traffic can be captured by anyone nearby, so strong authentication and encryption are essential. Modern networks use WPA2 or WPA3, either with a shared passphrase (Personal) or with individual credentials via 802.1X (Enterprise).",
[(B,"Generations","WEP: broken RC4-based encryption; never use. WPA: interim fix using TKIP. WPA2: AES-CCMP encryption; the long-time standard. WPA3: replaces PSK with SAE in Personal mode, requires Protected Management Frames (PMF), and offers a 192-bit security mode for Enterprise."),
 (I,"Personal vs Enterprise","Personal (PSK/SAE) uses one passphrase for everyone — simple, suitable for homes and small offices. Enterprise uses 802.1X: the client (supplicant) authenticates through the AP/WLC (authenticator) to a RADIUS server (authentication server) using an EAP method (e.g., PEAP, EAP-TLS, EAP-FAST). Each user has unique credentials and keys."),
 (X,"Why SAE matters","With WPA2-PSK, an attacker who captures the 4-way handshake can attempt an offline dictionary attack on the passphrase. WPA3's SAE (Simultaneous Authentication of Equals) resists offline dictionary attacks and provides forward secrecy. Open networks can use OWE (Enhanced Open) for encryption without a password. Configuring WPA2-PSK in the WLC GUI is covered in the WLAN GUI lesson.")],
"Weak Wi-Fi security exposes the entire internal network to anyone in the parking lot.",
[("Corporate","WPA2/WPA3-Enterprise with EAP-TLS certificates authenticates employees via Cisco ISE (RADIUS)."),
 ("Guest","A separate guest SSID uses WPA3-Personal or OWE and a captive portal, mapped to an internet-only VLAN.")],
["Choosing TKIP for WPA2 (use AES).",
 "Sharing one PSK with hundreds of employees in an enterprise.",
 "Assuming WPA3 Personal still uses the same PSK handshake as WPA2."],
[("PSK","Pre-Shared Key: common passphrase (WPA/WPA2 Personal)."),
 ("SAE","Simultaneous Authentication of Equals (WPA3 Personal)."),
 ("802.1X","Port-based network access control using EAP and RADIUS."),
 ("PMF","Protected Management Frames.")],
["WEP (RC4, broken) → WPA (TKIP) → WPA2 (AES-CCMP) → WPA3 (SAE, PMF, GCMP-256 in 192-bit mode).",
 "Enterprise = 802.1X + EAP + RADIUS; Personal = PSK/SAE.",
 "802.1X roles: supplicant, authenticator, authentication server."],
["Know which protocol uses which encryption and authentication method."],
"Use WPA2 (AES) or WPA3 (SAE, PMF). Personal mode uses a passphrase; Enterprise mode uses 802.1X/EAP with a RADIUS server for per-user authentication.",
[("Which encryption does WPA2 use?",["RC4","TKIP","AES-CCMP","DES"],2,"WPA2 uses AES-CCMP."),
 ("What does WPA3-Personal use instead of PSK?",["SAE","EAP-TLS","TKIP","WEP"],0,"Simultaneous Authentication of Equals."),
 ("In 802.1X, which role does the WLC/AP play?",["Supplicant","Authenticator","Authentication server","Certificate authority"],1,"The AP/WLC is the authenticator.")],
diagrams=["dot1x"])
